.\" Manpage for unit-health.
.\" Contact pfurpass@gmail.com to correct errors or typos.
.TH SERVICE-HEALTH 1 "2026-06-04" "unit-health 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
service\-health \- monitor systemd service health status
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B service\-health
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI [ SERVICE ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B service\-health
is a command\-line tool for monitoring the health of
.BR systemd (1)
services. With no arguments it prints a colored table of all services
showing their status, uptime, memory usage, CPU usage, restart count and
boot\-enable state. Given one or more
.I SERVICE
names, it restricts the output to those services.
.PP
The tool is designed to be dependency\-light (bash, systemd and coreutils
only), to work without an internet connection, and to degrade gracefully
when run without root privileges. Some figures (for example exact memory
accounting) may only be available to the superuser; missing values are shown
as a dash
.RB ( \- ).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B (no option)
Show all services in a table.
.TP
.I SERVICE ...
Show only the named services. A bare name such as
.I nginx
is treated as
.IR nginx.service .
.TP
.BR \-\-detailed " [" \fISERVICE\fR ]
Show a detailed view (description, PID, uptime, memory, CPU, restarts,
process tree and, for failed units, the last error). With no argument the
detailed view is produced for every service.
.TP
.B \-\-alerts
Show only services with problems or warnings (failed units, or running units
that exceed the configured CPU, memory or restart thresholds).
.TP
.B \-\-failed
Show only failed services together with their last error message.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Produce machine\-readable JSON. Each object contains the fields
.IR name ", " status ", " uptime_seconds ", " memory_mb ", " cpu_percent ", "
.IR restart_count_24h ", " enabled_on_boot " and " last_error .
Unavailable numeric values are rendered as
.BR null .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-colors
Disable ANSI colors. Colors are also disabled automatically when standard
output is not a terminal, when the
.B NO_COLOR
environment variable is set, or when
.B USE_COLORS=no
is configured.
.TP
.BR \-\-watch " [" \-\-interval " \fIN\fR]"
Live monitoring mode: refresh the table every
.I N
seconds (default 2). Exit with Ctrl+C.
.TP
.I SERVICE
.BR \-\-history " [" \-\-days " \fIN\fR]"
Show the restart and state\-change history for
.I SERVICE
over the last
.I N
days (default 7), derived from the journal.
.TP
.I SERVICE
.BR \-\-logs " [" \-\-lines " \fIN\fR] [" \-\-follow ]
Show the last
.I N
journal entries (default 10) for
.IR SERVICE .
With
.B \-\-follow
the output is tailed continuously, like
.BR "journalctl \-f" .
.TP
.I SERVICE
.B \-\-dependencies
Show what
.I SERVICE
requires, wants and is ordered against, plus the units that depend on it.
Failed hard dependencies are highlighted.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " FILE"
Load an alternative configuration file instead of
.IR /etc/service\-health/config.conf .
.TP
.B \-\-verbose
Print debugging information (such as the commands executed) to standard error.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B service\-health
Show all services.
.TP
.B service\-health nginx postgresql
Show only nginx and postgresql.
.TP
.B service\-health \-\-alerts
List only services that need attention.
.TP
.B service\-health \-\-json \-\-no\-colors
Emit JSON suitable for piping into another tool.
.TP
.B service\-health nginx \-\-logs \-\-lines 50
Show the last 50 journal lines for nginx.
.TP
.B service\-health mysql \-\-dependencies
Show the dependency graph around mysql.
.SH CONFIGURATION
On start\-up
.B service\-health
sources
.I /etc/service\-health/config.conf
if it exists and is readable. The file is a shell fragment that may set the
following variables:
.TP
.B IGNORE_SERVICES
Comma\- or whitespace\-separated list of service names to omit from output.
.TP
.B CPU_ALERT_THRESHOLD
CPU percentage above which a running service is flagged as a warning
(default 50).
.TP
.B MEMORY_ALERT_THRESHOLD
Memory in MB above which a running service is flagged as a warning
(default 1000).
.TP
.B RESTART_ALERT_THRESHOLD
Restart count above which a running service is flagged as a warning
(default 5).
.TP
.B USE_COLORS
Set to
.B no
to disable colored output by default.
.TP
.B DEFAULT_FORMAT
Default output format:
.IR table ", " json " or " detailed .
.TP
.B WATCH_INTERVAL
Default refresh interval for
.B \-\-watch
in seconds.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
Success (this includes the case of no services found).
.TP
.B 1
A requested service was not found, or systemd is unavailable.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command\-line usage.
.SH NOTES
The CPU percentage is the average over the lifetime of the process, as
reported by
.BR ps (1),
not an instantaneous sample. The restart count shown in the table is the
.B NRestarts
counter maintained by systemd since the unit was loaded; use
.B \-\-history
for a journal\-based view of restarts within a time window.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/service\-health/config.conf
Optional system\-wide configuration file.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR journalctl (1),
.BR systemd (1),
.BR ps (1)
